course: world
lesson: 60
title: Thinking it through — forces, heat, light and energy
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Scientists explain surprising things by using a few simple rules carefully, one step after another. This lesson puts together what you know about levers, heat, light, sound and energy.
explain: Heat moves from hotter things to colder things. Metals are good conductors of heat, so heat travels quickly along a metal spoon in hot tea up to your fingers; wood and plastic are insulators, so a wooden spoon stays cool at the top.
explain: A lever trades distance for force: if your end of the bar is ten times further from the pivot than the stone, you need about a tenth of the force, but your end must move ten times as far.
explain: Because light travels in straight lines, a shadow points away from the Sun. In the morning and evening the Sun is low, so shadows are long; at midday the Sun is highest, so shadows are shortest. As the Sun moves across the sky from east to west, shadows swing round from west to east.
explain: In a thunderstorm the light of the lightning reaches you almost at once, but the sound of thunder travels about one kilometre in three seconds — so counting the seconds between the flash and the bang tells how far away the storm is.
fact: Heat flows from hotter things to colder things.
fact: Metals conduct heat well, while wood, plastic, wool and air conduct it poorly.
fact: A lever with a long arm on your side lets a small force lift a heavy load, but your end moves further.
fact: Shadows are longest when the Sun is low and shortest when it is highest in the sky.
fact: In the morning the Sun is in the east, so shadows point west; in the evening they point east.
fact: Sound travels about one kilometre in three seconds through air, while light arrives almost instantly.
fact: Dark surfaces absorb more sunlight and warm up more than light-coloured ones.
fact: Air trapped in wool, feathers or double windows is a good insulator.
fact: No machine can give out more energy than is put into it.
exercise: Why does a metal spoon in hot tea get hot at the top, but a wooden spoon does not? | Metal conducts heat well and wood conducts it poorly | Wood is colder than metal to begin with, so it stays cool at the top | Tea only heats shiny things | Metal attracts heat like a magnet pulls on iron nails
exercise: A crowbar is 1 metre long and the pivot is 10 centimetres from the stone. Why can a child move the stone? | Her end is far from the pivot, so her small push becomes a big force | The crowbar makes the stone lighter as soon as the metal touches it | The pivot pushes the stone up by itself, so the child only has to steady the bar | Long bars cancel the stone's weight
exercise: When is your shadow shortest on a sunny day? | Around midday, when the Sun is highest | Early in the morning, when the Sun has just risen | Just before sunset, when the Sun is low in the west | At midnight, when the Moon is high in the sky
exercise: At 8 in the morning, which way does a tree's shadow point? | Towards the west, away from the morning Sun | Towards the east, straight at the morning Sun | Straight down under the tree | Towards the south always, whatever the time
exercise: You see lightning and count 9 seconds before the thunder. About how far away is the storm? | About 3 kilometres | About 9 kilometres | About 1 kilometre | About 27 kilometres
exercise: Why does a woolly jumper keep you warm? | It traps air, which slows down the loss of your body heat | It makes heat of its own as the wool rubs against your skin | It pulls warmth in from the cold air | Wool is a strong conductor of heat
exercise: Why do saucepans often have metal bodies but plastic or wooden handles? | Metal passes heat to the food, the handle stops it reaching your hand | Plastic handles make the food cook faster because they hold the heat inside the pan | Metal handles would be too light | Wooden handles conduct heat into the pan
exercise: Why does a car parked in the sun get hotter if it is black rather than white? | Black absorbs more sunlight and turns it into heat | White paint makes its own coolness that keeps the inside cold | Black cars have thinner metal that lets the heat through faster | Sunlight only warms cars that have their windows closed tight
exercise: An inventor says his machine gives out more energy than it takes in. What should you think? | It cannot be true, energy is never made from nothing | It is true if the machine is big | It is true if it uses magnets | It is true if it runs very fast
exercise: To lift a heavy rock with a lever, you push your end down 1 metre and the rock rises 10 centimetres. What did you gain? | You needed much less force than the rock's weight | You got more energy than you put in | The rock lost most of its weight | You moved the rock further than your hand
exercise: Why do double-glazed windows keep a house warm? | The gap of air between the panes is a poor conductor of heat | The double glass shines more sunlight in to warm up the rooms | Two panes make the room darker, so it holds on to more warmth | Glass conducts heat better than air, so it carries warmth inside
exercise: Why does a metal bench feel colder to sit on than a wooden one on a cold day, though both are at the same temperature? | Metal carries heat away from your body faster | The metal bench is really much colder than the wooden one | Wood makes its own heat | Metal reflects the warmth of the Sun
test: A cook leaves a metal spoon and a plastic spoon in a pot of hot soup for five minutes. Which handle is safe to hold? | The plastic one, because plastic is a poor conductor of heat | The metal one, because metal stays cool while the soup is hot | Both, because heat cannot move up a spoon | Neither, because plastic is the better conductor
test: A shadow stick is put in the ground. During the day its shadow first shortens, then grows again. Why? | The Sun climbs higher until midday, then sinks lower | The stick grows and shrinks with the heat | The Earth moves closer to the Sun at noon | Clouds make shadows change their length
test: You count 6 seconds between a lightning flash and its thunder, and later only 3 seconds. What has happened? | The storm has come closer | The storm has moved further away | The thunder has become faster | The lightning has become slower
test: Two children try to move a boulder with the same bar. One holds it near the pivot, the other at the far end. Who needs less force? | The one holding the far end | The one holding near the pivot | They need exactly the same force | Neither, since bars do not help
test: Why do polar bears' thick fur and fat help them survive the Arctic? | They trap heat in and slow down its escape into the cold | They make heat from the snow that sticks to the bears' coats | They let the cold flow quickly into the body to toughen it | They reflect the cold back into the air before it reaches the skin
test: An evening walker in the countryside sees his long shadow stretching ahead of him. Which way is he walking? | East, away from the setting Sun | West, towards the setting Sun | North, towards the Pole Star | He could be walking any way at all
test: Which is true about a lever that lets you lift a heavy load with a small push? | Your end has to move a longer distance | It creates extra energy from nothing | The load becomes truly lighter | Your end moves a shorter distance
test: On a hot summer day, why do people in sunny countries often paint houses white? | White reflects sunlight, so the houses stay cooler | White paint makes cold air | White walls absorb more heat to store it | White houses are heavier and thicker
test: A thermos keeps tea hot for hours. What is the main idea of how it works? | It stops heat flowing out from the hot tea to the colder air | It makes new heat inside the flask | It keeps the cold from flowing in like water | It uses electricity from the tea
test: why's the metal spoon in my tea hot but the wooden one not | Metal conducts heat well, wood conducts it poorly | Wood is colder inside, it's like full of tiny ice bits | Metal makes its own heat whenever it gets dunked in tea | The tea just likes metal more, it sticks to it harder
test: evening, sun going down, which way's my shadow pointing | East | West | North | South
test: my brother says a coat makes heat to keep you warm. true? | No, air trapped in it slows the heat leaving your body | Yes, coats make their own heat | Yes, wool burns slowly | No, coats only keep rain off
test: a classmate says cold flows from the ice into your hand. right? | No, heat flows from your warm hand into the ice | Yes, cold moves into warm things, that's why your hand hurts | Yes, ice gives out cold | No, nothing flows at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the direction of the flow of heat? | Heat flows from the hotter body to the colder body | Heat flows from the colder body to the hotter body | Heat flows only between bodies of equal temperature | Heat flows exclusively through metals
exam: It is given that the Sun stands in the east at morning. Determine the direction in which shadows shall then point. | Towards the west | Towards the east | Towards the north | Directly downwards
exam: It is given that lightning is observed and the thunder is heard six seconds subsequently. Ascertain the approximate distance of the storm. | About two kilometres | About six kilometres | About eighteen kilometres | About half a kilometre
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the output of energy of any machine. | No machine gives out more energy than is put into it | A well-built machine gives out more energy than it receives | A machine creates energy whenever it is in motion | A lever gives out twice the energy put into it
